The course will cover all key aspects of using the Geant4 toolkit in for any type of applications, including:
  - how to create complex geometry setups, and tips for creating complicated shapes (solids);
  - how to describe electromagnetic and other fields and adjust the accuracy of tracking particles in them;
  - how to extract energy deposition and other observables in multiple ways;
  - how to speed up simulation though use of cuts (secondary thresholds);
  - exploring the use ‘event-biasing’ to suppress less important tracks (for appropriate applications);
  - using ‘fast’ parameterisations (traditional or ML) in selected portions of a setup;
  - adapting an applications for multi-threading;
  - how to customise the user interface and create custom commands to configure your application.

The course will also cover the physics capabilities and options of Geant4, to enable you to
  - gain a first understanding of the physics model available (and their strengths and relative performance costs)
  - understand the physics configuration options (key physics lists and their variants)
  - navigate the process of choosing an appropriate physics list, and how to validate it (or find existing validation)
  - identify alternative physics configurations (physics lists or parameter variation) for systematic studies.

Extra emphasis will be paid to aspects most relevant to HEP and Nuclear Physics experiments, but most of the topics covered will be relevant for diverse application areas.

Places for in person participation will be limited to 30. Some are reserved for CERN participants; the rest will be available on a first come, first serve basis. 
Online participation (via Zoom) is also available.

The cost to attend the course (for those involved in CERN or other HEP experiments or projects) are:
 - via videoconference  50 Swiss Francs for employed in universities / HEP and 25 for students;
 - in person it is 75 Swiss Francs for employed in universities / HEP and 50 for students.

Registration is required.
